The Core Experiment: Two Data Scenarios
The script prepares two versions of the exact same dataset to see how the results change:
* Scenario A (Data Only): It groups regions purely by their statistical profile (e.g., average behaviors, volatility, patterns over time). In this scenario, a region in Europe and a region in Asia could end up in the same cluster if their data looks identical.
* Scenario B (Data + Space): It appends latitude and longitude directly to that statistical profile. This explicitly forces the algorithms to treat physical proximity as a key factor. Regions that are far apart are much less likely to end up in the same group.


The Algorithmic Validation: Two Strategy Types
To ensure the groups aren't just a fluke of one specific mathematical method, the script runs both scenarios through two distinct types of clustering strategies:
* Hierarchical Clustering (Bottom-Up): It looks at the data like a family tree, continuously pairing the two most similar regions until they form larger branches (clusters).
* K-Means Clustering (Center-Out): It looks at the data space globally, picks center points (centroids), and draws boundaries around them so that every region belongs to the closest center.


Methodology: Statistical Feature-Based Clustering
The methodological approach implemented in this workflow does not analyze raw data in its original temporal or point-by-point dimension. Instead, it adopts a Feature-Based Clustering framework.
This process extracts a mathematical "fingerprint" for each region, compressing complex, dynamic behaviors into a compact set of statistical descriptors.


Why this Approach is Optimal from a Temporal Perspective
When dealing with data collected over time (time-series), clustering raw sequences directly introduces massive inefficiencies and mathematical traps. The feature-based approach is highly optimal from a temporal standpoint for three main reasons:
* Phase and Time-Shift Invariance: If Region A and Region B experience the exact same structural pattern (e.g., a massive spike in activity), but Region B's pattern happens two weeks later due to seasonal shifts, raw clustering treats them as completely different. A feature-based approach extracts the nature of the behavior (the volatility and shape of the spike) rather than its exact date, correctly grouping them together.
* Massive Dimensionality Reduction: Clustering a raw time-series with hundreds or thousands of daily timestamps creates a "curse of dimensionality," making algorithms like K-Means slow and inaccurate. Compressing the timeline into 9 robust metrics speeds up calculations exponentially and ensures the algorithm doesn't get lost in infinite variables.
* Temporal Noise Filtering: Time-series data is notoriously noisy, filled with daily random anomalies that do not reflect the true nature of a region. By calculating global properties (like entropy or historical memory), we filter out day-to-day background static, forcing the model to cluster based on long-term structural dynamics.
